**Read:** [Production of Chlor-alkali, BREF CAK](../sources/Chlor-Alkali-Production-BREF.lingua#r_69QNQ29VT4NA2MXHTVWDNP8T2W), the whole 2014 BREF including preparation, cells, product conditioning, conversion and site legacy; [Inorganic Chemical Technology — Nanda Kishore, NPTEL](../sources/Inorganic-Chemical-Technology-Kishore.lingua#r_77Z8T5RFYFNNXZ7XKZFKPRKDKR), the complete chlor-alkali teaching; [Chemical Reactor Analysis and Design Fundamentals — Rawlings and Ekerdt](../sources/Chemical-Reactor-Analysis-Rawlings-Ekerdt.lingua#r_7YQAV27Y3J0B537Z6K5HDN8H0A), the full relevant reaction/material and energy-account reasoning.

The ideal net reaction 2NaCl+2H₂O→Cl₂+H₂+2NaOH links salt/water, electricity and three useful chemical products. The cell's actual separator/membrane, electrode reactions, current efficiency, voltage, brine condition and temperature influence production and purity. One co-product cannot be counted without its connected chemical account.

Separate reversible electrical work, measured cell input and wider system duties. Brine preparation/return, caustic concentration, chlorine conditioning and hydrogen purification/compression require material, energy, equipment and maintenance. A high current efficiency does not by itself establish overall accepted-product efficiency.

Different historical cell routes carry different impurity and legacy issues; a dated public reference must be read with its actual scope. Product demand and transfer requirements also constrain the useful balance between chlorine, caustic and hydrogen. A co-product is valuable under specified quality and destination, not simply because the equation produces it.

**Work:** reconstruct the entire public membrane-cell route and independently close sodium, chlorine, hydrogen, water, charge and energy accounts. Compare a documented alternative or change at the same accepted co-product boundaries. Explain cell/material state, purification, service history, measurement uncertainty and residual obligations through a full operating interval.

